From ad24702309fbce90f0f4f85321de65d53ae25b0b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yvan Janssens <yvanj@cyber.gent>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 12:40:12 +0100
Subject: [RFC PATCH 3/7] KVM: s390: Present interruptions in ESA/390 format in
 the mode

A CPU in the ESA/390-compatibility mode takes interruptions the ESA/390
way (Principles of Operation, "Assigned Storage Locations" and chapter 6,
"Interruption Action"): the old and new PSWs are eight bytes long and lie
at real 0-127 (restart 8/0, external 24/88, supervisor call 32/96, program
40/104, machine check 48/112, input/output 56/120), and the interruption
parameters that are wider than four bytes in z/Architecture have their
ESA/390 places.

The machine presents the interruptions it recognizes inside SIE in that
format by itself. Make the ones KVM presents follow:

- all places that store an old PSW or fetch a new one go through
  kvm_s390_lc_store_old_psw(), kvm_s390_lc_read_new_psw() and
  kvm_s390_lc_load_new_psw(), which map the location and convert the PSW
  when the vCPU is in the mode. That covers external, input/output and
  restart interruptions, whose parameters have the same locations in both
  modes, and the checks of a new PSW in the interception handlers;
- program interruptions: PER address (152) and monitor code (156) are four
  bytes long, a protection exception stores its identification at 144, no
  breaking-event address is stored. Exceptions of dynamic address
  translation cannot occur;
- machine checks: architectural-mode identification 0, the CPU timer and
  clock comparator at 216 and 224, and the register save areas of the
  first 512 bytes with 32-bit general and control registers and
  floating-point registers 0, 2, 4 and 6;
- the interruption parameters of a program interception have the layout
  of an ESA/390-format state description, with four-byte fields. That
  layout is what the machine was observed to store; no public document
  describing it is known;
- a PSW with the DAT bit or the extended-addressing-mode bit is not valid
  in the mode, and EXTRACT PSW shows bit 12 as one.

No functional change as long as no vCPU is in the mode.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5-2
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
